{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786210149,"nonce":"XDGvkUpWsNF8ITBP+kxVUrxIHLM=","target":"fa94e20f9fc785d39b7c09affb3cee7390a8bc87061fd76b9ad0d0d5cebd7f3c","message":"**What is wrong.** `internal/ingestion/domain/bounds.go` defines B1–B17 as named\nconstants — 8 MiB body, 64 labels, 4 KiB values, the B12/B13 windows, B17\nchunking — and the conformance audit marked layer 2 of the validation\narchitecture \"PASS\" purely on their existence (\"all of B1…B17 present as named\nconstants with literal values\", `review-conformance.md` §5). There is no\n`*_test.go` under `internal/ingestion/` at all. Each bound is a rejection\nbehaviour with a status code and a rejection record, and none of that is asserted.\n\n**Why it matters.** The ingest endpoint is the product's front door and the one\nsurface an untrusted third party can reach with a body. A bound that is off by one\neither loses alerts (rejecting a legitimate payload with a 4xx that Alertmanager\nnever retries) or fails to protect the process. The bounds are also the documented\ncontract in ADR 0007, which a customer's Alertmanager depends on.\n\n**Where.** `internal/ingestion/domain/bounds.go`,\n`internal/ingestion/service/accept.go`, `internal/ingestion/api/`.\n\n**Done when.** Each of B1–B17 has a test that submits a payload sitting exactly\non and exactly over the bound, and asserts the status code, the error code, the\n`rejected_alerts` accounting and whether the batch as a whole is still accepted —\nincluding B17 chunk boundaries with a batch that spans several chunks.","files":null}]}